      "description": "Prince Mojmír I unifies the Slavic tribes of Moravia (833); his successor Rastislav resists Frankish domination and invites the Byzantine missionaries Cyril and Methodius (863), who create the Glagolitic alphabet and Slavonic liturgy, a pivotal moment in Slavic cultural history. Svatopluk I (871–894) expands Great Moravia to its greatest extent, encompassing Bohemia, Slovakia, Pannonia, and possibly Poland.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q193152",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q193152"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Great Moravia",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Moravia"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "phase": "collapse",
      "years": "894–907",
      "description": "Svatopluk I's death triggers a succession dispute among his three sons; Great Moravia fragments while simultaneously facing Magyar (Hungarian) migration and raids from the east. The Magyars' settlement of the Pannonian plain (895–900) cuts Great Moravia off from the Balkans. Three major Magyar invasions (899, 900, 906) culminate in the complete destruction of Great Moravia by 907.",
      "sources": [
        {
